00:07:31,120 --> 00:07:34,160
of goodness towards the world, right?

135
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:38,740
And so we're created in the image of this God that still holds his posture towards the world

136
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:40,280
that it's good, it's full of goodness.

137
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:46,820
But this, it's hard to see, right?

138
00:07:46,820 --> 00:07:56,000
You know, it's not hard to find pain and it's not hard to find the bad things in the world.

139
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:06,880
So how in the world do we as Christians lay hold of our identity as image bearers of a God

140
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:08,160
who sees the world as good?

141
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:14,120
I think Jesus has some wisdom for us, as he usually does.

142
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:17,820
Let's go to Matthew chapter six together.

143
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:24,120
This is in the middle of Jesus' Sermon on the Mount.

144
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:28,520
If you're not familiar with this teaching, this is the largest teaching that Jesus gives

145
00:08:28,520 --> 00:08:31,660
at one time in his ministry.

146
00:08:31,660 --> 00:08:33,300
It's like his greatest hits.

147
00:08:33,300 --> 00:08:34,040
It's pretty awesome.

148
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:41,300
And so in the middle of this sermon that Jesus gives, he hits this part that says, lay up for

149
00:08:41,300 --> 00:08:43,020
yourselves treasures in heaven.

150
00:08:43,020 --> 00:08:46,820
Don't lay up for yourselves treasures on earth.

151
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:52,940
And the idea behind that is that don't spend your time working for things that are going

152
00:08:52,940 --> 00:08:53,900
to perish anyway.

153
00:08:53,900 --> 00:08:58,560
Don't exhaust yourselves accumulating things that are going to go away, but spend your time

154
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:03,820
focusing on things that have eternal ramifications, namely loving God and loving the people around

155
00:09:03,820 --> 00:09:04,900
you, right?

156
00:09:04,900 --> 00:09:09,600
And so then we hit this verse that goes like this.

157
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:10,980
The eye is the lamp of the body.

158
00:09:10,980 --> 00:09:14,720
If your eyes are good, your whole body will be full of light.

159
00:09:15,460 --> 00:09:18,960
But if your eyes are bad, your whole body will be full of darkness.

160
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:25,440
If the light within you is darkness, how great is that darkness?

161
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:29,340
To which we all respond, what in the world are you talking about?

162
00:09:29,340 --> 00:09:30,240
Right?

163
00:09:30,240 --> 00:09:32,860
This hits us as being a little bit weird, right?

164
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:39,600
But at the time that Jesus gave this teaching, this wouldn't have been considered a weird teaching

165
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:41,320
or this obscure teaching.

166
00:09:41,320 --> 00:09:44,880
This was a known kind of thought process at the time.

167
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:48,420
It's the idea of having a good eye versus a bad eye.

168
00:09:48,420 --> 00:09:56,580
The Hebrew for this would be an ayin tovah, an eye that's oriented towards good, or an ayin ra'ah

169
00:09:56,580 --> 00:09:58,140
is the inverse of that.

170
00:09:58,140 --> 00:10:00,280
It's an eye that's oriented towards bad.

171
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:07,740
And so a person with a good eye will see other people as those that bear the image of God

172
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:12,680
and will understand that we live in a world that has enough, that it's an abundant world,

173
00:10:12,680 --> 00:10:18,180
that there's enough to go around so we can live with generosity and we can live with open hands

174
00:10:18,180 --> 00:10:20,000
and we can live with hope and joy.

175
00:10:20,540 --> 00:10:26,640
And on the other side, there's this idea of a bad eye, an ayin ra'ah, and somebody that

176
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:37,420
has this view towards life sees the world as a dog-eat-dog place, like the natural bent

177
00:10:37,420 --> 00:10:42,540
is towards the negative, that there's only so much resource to go around, and so I have

178
00:10:42,540 --> 00:10:46,340
to do everything that I can to collect everything that I want and need.

179
00:10:46,340 --> 00:10:48,700
And humans aren't an image bearer of God.

180
00:10:48,700 --> 00:10:53,240
They're an obstacle that gets in my way as I try and accumulate the things that I want

181
00:10:53,240 --> 00:10:54,800
or need, right?

182
00:10:54,800 --> 00:10:59,480
So when Jesus says, the eye is the lamp of the body, if your eyes are good, your whole

183
00:10:59,480 --> 00:11:04,300
body will be full of light, what he's saying is, if you turn your gaze towards knowing that

184
00:11:04,300 --> 00:11:09,080
there's enough in the world and that you know who your heavenly father is and he takes care

185
00:11:09,080 --> 00:11:12,680
of you, your whole body is going to be full of light and the people around you are going

186
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:14,140
to see that, right?

187
00:11:14,280 --> 00:11:20,820
But if your eyes are bad and you look towards the negative and you think that there's not

188
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:25,640
enough to go around and you have to fight for everything that you get, how great is that

189
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:26,640
darkness, he says.

190
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:29,760
Right?

191
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:32,140
We do this with our kids.

192
00:11:32,140 --> 00:11:33,040
It's a gratitude practice.

193
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:33,520
It's really cute.

194
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:36,160
We've got twins that are eight.

195
00:11:36,160 --> 00:11:41,700
And so when we lay them down in bed at night, we say, give us an ayin tovah from the day.

196
00:11:41,700 --> 00:11:45,440
And, you know, they're eight, so they say all kinds of weird stuff.

197
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:47,660
Ayin tovah that my brother's feet weren't smelly today.

198
00:11:47,660 --> 00:11:48,540
You know, that kind of thing.

199
00:11:48,540 --> 00:11:52,200
But then they'll hit the spot and they'll just say beautiful things.

200
00:11:52,200 --> 00:11:55,620
Ayin tovah that I have a family that loves me.

201
00:11:56,300 --> 00:12:00,300
And ayin tovah that we got to eat three meals today.

202
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:01,360
Right?

203
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:12,520
And this doesn't mean, this isn't like, this isn't pathologic optimism to have a good eye.

204
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:13,200
Right?

205
00:12:13,200 --> 00:12:18,020
We don't turn our eyes away from the things that are bad in the world.

206
00:12:18,160 --> 00:12:21,360
But we start from a place where we see the good in other people.

207
00:12:21,360 --> 00:12:26,780
And then we can engage those areas of darkness and bring some hope and peace into them.

208
00:12:26,780 --> 00:12:27,840
Right?

209
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:37,440
So, the next part of the Scripture, it goes on in the Sermon on the Mount.

210
00:12:38,100 --> 00:12:44,260
And Jesus begins to talk about not worrying, which I think is beautiful here.

211
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:48,980
He says, don't worry about your life, what you'll eat or drink, or about your body, what you're going to wear.

212
00:12:48,980 --> 00:12:50,620
Life's more important than food.

213
00:12:50,620 --> 00:12:52,400
The body's more important than clothes.

214
00:12:52,400 --> 00:12:54,840
Look at flowers.

215
00:12:54,840 --> 00:12:57,080
They don't worry.

216
00:12:57,080 --> 00:12:58,900
They don't work.

217
00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:01,340
But nobody's dressed like those.

218
00:13:01,340 --> 00:13:02,120
Right?

219
00:13:02,120 --> 00:13:03,720
And look at birds.

220
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:06,840
They don't worry about what their next meal is going to be, what they're going to eat.

221
00:13:06,840 --> 00:13:08,440
But God continues to feed them.

222
00:13:08,440 --> 00:13:10,900
And aren't you more important than birds and flowers?

223
00:13:10,900 --> 00:13:14,780
Your heavenly Father knows you, and he's going to take care of you.

224
00:13:14,780 --> 00:13:24,280
And I know in our congregation that we've got people who live in abundance and that have everything that they need and more.

225
00:13:24,820 --> 00:13:32,120
And I also know that we have people in our congregation that legitimately don't know what their next meal is going to be or how they're going to pay their next rent.

226
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:41,540
And I don't, what I will tell you is that I know our heavenly Father.

227
00:13:41,540 --> 00:13:45,800
And I know that he knows you and that he loves you.

228
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:49,900
And he's going to give you everything that you need.

229
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:53,840
And it probably won't look like the way that you want it to.

230
00:13:54,820 --> 00:13:57,160
And you might not get what you want, but he knows you.

231
00:13:57,160 --> 00:13:59,500
There is enough, and he will take care of you.

232
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:04,060
So what does this have to do with Sabbath?

233
00:14:04,060 --> 00:14:04,720
Great question.

234
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:06,340
I was wondering that myself.

235
00:14:06,340 --> 00:14:18,840
God created this, and he knows that left to our own devices that our natural instinct is to turn our gaze towards the negative.

236
00:14:19,440 --> 00:14:26,760
The statistics that I was reading on this say that we have negative thoughts 70 to 80% of the time.

237
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:29,420
Like that's our natural bent toward life.

238
00:14:29,420 --> 00:14:35,620
And so God sets up this time of Sabbath and puts boundaries around it and says,

239
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:41,840
this is your time to take your thoughts captive and to turn them towards good things, right?

240
00:14:41,840 --> 00:14:45,140
This is the reason that we delight on Sabbath.

241
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:54,460
On Sabbath, we make intentional choices to see the good world that God has put us in,

242
00:14:54,860 --> 00:14:58,540
knowing that he gives us everything that we need.

243
00:14:58,540 --> 00:15:06,220
So for the last couple minutes of the time here today, I want to give some practical tips to this.

244
00:15:06,220 --> 00:15:09,700
I told you guys that my family and I started this three or four years ago,

245
00:15:09,700 --> 00:15:16,720
and it's been interesting sorting out what it looks like in our lives, right?

246
00:15:16,720 --> 00:15:22,220
But I think there are some things that are applicable across the board here,

247
00:15:22,220 --> 00:15:25,780
that if we can do some of these, it might help you make some space in your life.

248
00:15:25,780 --> 00:15:28,440
So Brian's practical tips on Sabbath.

249
00:15:28,440 --> 00:15:33,980
Make a list, take some time during the wake.

250
00:15:33,980 --> 00:15:39,500
Make a list of things that you will do on Sabbath and that you won't do on Sabbath, right?

251
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:45,040
So this might look like on Sabbath, I will take a nap.

252
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:48,020
On Sabbath, I will eat dessert.

253
00:15:48,020 --> 00:15:50,040
Come on.

254
00:15:50,040 --> 00:15:52,400
Somebody in the back.

255
00:15:52,400 --> 00:15:52,780
Awesome.

256
00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:58,320
Make a list of things that bring you joy and that speak to your soul,

257
00:15:58,320 --> 00:15:59,840
that draw you closer to your creator.

258
00:15:59,840 --> 00:16:01,680
Make a list of I will not.

259
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:04,600
I will not do my typical work.

260
00:16:04,600 --> 00:16:07,780
I will not do chores that don't bring me joy.

261
00:16:08,240 --> 00:16:09,920
Those kind of things, right?

262
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:11,980
Make preparations.

263
00:16:11,980 --> 00:16:16,060
We screwed this up so bad when we first started practicing Sabbath.

264
00:16:16,060 --> 00:16:20,020
We really like to dive in with both feet here.

265
00:16:20,020 --> 00:16:23,180
So we decided that we were going to keep traditional Hebrew Sabbath,

266
00:16:23,180 --> 00:16:26,060
meaning that we were going to start at 20 minutes to sundown on Friday

267
00:16:26,060 --> 00:16:28,620
and that we were going to carry it through until Saturday evening.

268
00:16:28,620 --> 00:16:31,660
And so my wife and I both work full time.

269
00:16:31,660 --> 00:16:33,520
We've got kids that are in school.

270
00:16:33,520 --> 00:16:37,820
And so what this looked like in our house is that we would rush in from a busy week at work.

271
00:16:37,820 --> 00:16:39,380
We'd throw some food on the table.

272
00:16:39,380 --> 00:16:41,120
We'd put my kids to bed.

273
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:44,820
And then my wife and I would look around the disaster of our home and tap our foot

274
00:16:44,820 --> 00:16:48,860
and wonder when we could get about the business of putting our life back together.

275
00:16:48,860 --> 00:16:50,180
And it was awful.

276
00:16:50,180 --> 00:16:51,840
So, like, don't do that.

277
00:16:54,600 --> 00:17:00,940
Now, we've scooted our Sabbath to Saturday evening to Sunday evening.

278
00:17:00,940 --> 00:17:08,020
And we spend the entirety of Saturday preparing for the following day so that we can actually enjoy it.

279
00:17:08,220 --> 00:17:11,000
So take some time and prepare your day.

280
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:17,860
The next thing that I would say is find some ways to mark the days, put some boundaries around it,

281
00:17:17,860 --> 00:17:24,920
and be intentional about doing things to say, now is when our Sabbath time starts and now is when our Sabbath time ends.

282
00:17:25,280 --> 00:17:28,520
We want to devote this time to God.

283
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:31,400
And so we mark the ends of it.

284
00:17:31,400 --> 00:17:33,520
This could be singing a song.

285
00:17:33,520 --> 00:17:34,960
This could be saying a prayer.

286
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:36,900
This could be lighting candles.

287
00:17:36,900 --> 00:17:38,140
That's what we do in our home.

288
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:44,200
But make sure that we're very intentional about putting these boundaries around the day.

289
00:17:44,200 --> 00:17:49,060
So we've got marking the day to put boundaries around it.

290
00:17:49,060 --> 00:17:53,020
We've got this list of I wills and I won'ts while we're in the day.

291
00:17:53,020 --> 00:18:00,260
And then the next thing that I would say is take some time and intentionally put away worry.

292
00:18:00,260 --> 00:18:06,880
There's this practice that I've heard people talk about with this, something called a Sabbath box,

293
00:18:06,880 --> 00:18:08,500
which I think is a cool idea.

294
00:18:08,800 --> 00:18:10,880
We don't do it, but I think it's a great idea.

295
00:18:10,880 --> 00:18:16,860
The idea here is that you take some time, you write down the things that you're anxious about

296
00:18:16,860 --> 00:18:18,320
or the things that you're worried about.

297
00:18:18,320 --> 00:18:24,300
You put them in a box along with probably your electronic devices, and then you put it away.

298
00:18:24,300 --> 00:18:32,160
So we're symbolically and very practically separating ourselves from the worries that we have.

299
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:38,040
And then do the things that bring you joy.

300
00:18:38,600 --> 00:18:41,160
And the list here is long.

301
00:18:41,160 --> 00:18:42,900
Take a nap.

302
00:18:42,900 --> 00:18:44,220
Eat dessert.

303
00:18:44,220 --> 00:18:45,400
Take a hike.

304
00:18:45,400 --> 00:18:47,960
Play games with your family.

305
00:18:47,960 --> 00:18:48,580
Sing.

306
00:18:48,580 --> 00:18:49,400
Dance.

307
00:18:49,400 --> 00:18:49,860
Draw.

308
00:18:49,860 --> 00:18:55,200
Do the things that draw you closer to God that make your spirit feel alive.

309
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:01,240
There's a tendency to think that Sabbath is a day where you sit around and don't do anything.

310
00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:02,760
That's not what it looks like in our house.

311
00:19:02,760 --> 00:19:09,140
I mean, we're very active on Sabbath, but we're active doing the things that bring us to light

312
00:19:09,140 --> 00:19:10,440
and that draw us closer to God.

313
00:19:10,440 --> 00:19:12,040
This is the idea, right?

314
00:19:13,240 --> 00:19:17,460
The other thing that I would say is while you're doing these things, take your time with them.

315
00:19:20,260 --> 00:19:25,260
I do a lot of things throughout the week that should bring me joy but don't because I rush through them.

316
00:19:25,980 --> 00:19:33,080
Like, I'll eat this beautiful meal, but I always eat like I'm at boot camp and I don't really taste the food that I'm eating, right?

317
00:19:33,460 --> 00:19:36,840
And so, on Sabbath, chew your food, right?

318
00:19:36,840 --> 00:19:40,740
It's great, right?

319
00:19:40,740 --> 00:19:47,580
When you go out and take a walk, pick up one of those maple leaves and throw it up in the air and see that thing spiral down and say,

320
00:19:47,580 --> 00:19:49,480
what a creative God we serve.

321
00:19:49,480 --> 00:19:54,360
Like, take your time on the day and enjoy the things that God gives you.

322
00:19:54,360 --> 00:20:02,120
And then be mindful of the work that God's doing in your heart and in your soul as you do all these things.

323
00:20:03,460 --> 00:20:06,020
He's going to do some work on you.

324
00:20:06,020 --> 00:20:18,000
As I was preparing for today, I was thinking about if I wanted to use our family as a case study, which, you know, here we go.

325
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:24,660
But I thought I'd talk you through what, you know, the 48 hours around Sabbath actually looks like for us

326
00:20:24,660 --> 00:20:30,340
because, like I said, it's hard to see how it might work in your own space.

327
00:20:30,340 --> 00:20:32,260
And this is how we've settled into it.

328
00:20:32,260 --> 00:20:34,720
It's probably going to look different in five years for us.

329
00:20:34,720 --> 00:20:37,520
But this is how it looks for right now.

330
00:20:37,520 --> 00:20:45,960
We get up on Saturday morning and we tidy up any loose ends from work that we have from the last week.

331
00:20:45,960 --> 00:20:47,280
We grocery shop.

332
00:20:47,280 --> 00:20:50,220
We clean our house and do our laundry.

333
00:20:50,220 --> 00:20:52,240
And then we prepare for our meal.

334
00:20:52,240 --> 00:20:53,420
Like, this is our Saturday.

335
00:20:55,720 --> 00:21:03,020
And then on Saturday night, we've had the same, roughly the same group of people coming over to our house for the last several years.

336
00:21:03,020 --> 00:21:04,400
We share a meal with them every Saturday.

337
00:21:04,400 --> 00:21:15,560
And so when everybody comes into the house, we sit at our table and we take a couple minutes to be quiet and to breathe and to put worry away.

338
00:21:17,340 --> 00:21:19,220
And then we light two candles.

339
00:21:19,220 --> 00:21:26,420
One candle represents God's command to us to remember the Sabbath.

340
00:21:26,420 --> 00:21:31,000
And then the other command to set it apart or make it holy.

341
00:21:31,800 --> 00:21:37,620
And when we do this, we say traditional Hebrew prayers for Sabbath in Hebrew.

342
00:21:37,620 --> 00:21:42,300
And it's super weird and awkward for people who haven't been at our house before.

343
00:21:42,300 --> 00:21:42,840
And I'll warn them.

344
00:21:42,840 --> 00:21:43,940
I'm like, it's about to get weird.

345
00:21:43,940 --> 00:21:45,200
And then it does.

346
00:21:47,260 --> 00:21:51,360
And the point of it isn't just to appropriate another culture, which, you know.

347
00:21:51,360 --> 00:22:01,360
But the point is to make it strange enough for my kids that they know that what's happening for the next 24 hours is different than what we do any other time in the week.

348
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:02,220
Right?

349
00:22:02,220 --> 00:22:04,540
So we want to mark this day.

350
00:22:04,540 --> 00:22:06,440
And so we do strange stuff to do it.

351
00:22:06,440 --> 00:22:09,000
We light our two candles.

352
00:22:09,820 --> 00:22:18,800
And then we hold up our drinks and we say another Hebrew prayer and we thank God for the fruit of the vine, understanding that it comes from him.

353
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:29,220
And then we hold up our food and we say, thank you, God, for bringing forth bread from the earth, that whatever we do with our hands doesn't matter if it's not blessed by God.

354
00:22:29,220 --> 00:22:30,100
He brings the blessing.

355
00:22:30,100 --> 00:22:36,180
And then we light a candle for each of my children and we bless them.

356
00:22:36,260 --> 00:22:42,300
And we say, Ezra, may God make you like Ephraim and Manasseh and Eliza, may God make you like Sarah, Rebecca, Rachel, and Leah.

357
00:22:42,300 --> 00:22:45,180
And then we eat and we enjoy each other's company.

358
00:22:45,180 --> 00:22:54,200
And what I know is that Sabbath by yourself is pretty great, but Sabbath in community is a lot better.

359
00:22:54,200 --> 00:22:54,740
Right?

360
00:22:54,740 --> 00:23:01,140
So I would encourage you as you're finding times to implement this, find some people to do it with.

361
00:23:01,140 --> 00:23:02,040
It's a lot better.

362
00:23:02,040 --> 00:23:04,220
And we eat dessert.

363
00:23:04,680 --> 00:23:11,040
And my kids, if you ask them why we eat dessert on Sabbath, they'll say, because God's love is sweet.

364
00:23:11,040 --> 00:23:11,700
Right?

365
00:23:11,700 --> 00:23:18,860
It's this hook to remind us that God gives us good things and that he loves us.

366
00:23:18,860 --> 00:23:21,380
And then we go to bed.

367
00:23:21,380 --> 00:23:23,300
We come here the next day, worship with you people.

368
00:23:23,940 --> 00:23:28,720
And then we go home and we take naps and we play games and we read books and we delight.

369
00:23:28,720 --> 00:23:32,400
And then on Sunday night, we make a very simple meal.

370
00:23:32,900 --> 00:23:38,200
Usually, it's sheet pan nachos, which we've affectionately started calling chabachos.

371
00:23:38,200 --> 00:23:44,280
So we eat our chabachos and we watch a movie and that's how we finish our day.

372
00:23:44,280 --> 00:23:44,920
Right?

373
00:23:44,920 --> 00:23:45,800
This is our Sabbath.

374
00:23:45,800 --> 00:23:50,520
So again, it's going to look different for you and for your family and for your time.

375
00:23:50,760 --> 00:23:58,720
But I would encourage you guys to flesh it out and try this practice because God does work through it.

376
00:24:00,720 --> 00:24:02,440
David, you want to come up and play the piano?

377
00:24:02,440 --> 00:24:04,100
I've been looking forward to this part.

378
00:24:04,100 --> 00:24:07,660
Mostly because I love hearing David play the piano.

379
00:24:07,660 --> 00:24:11,300
But like it always sounds so cool when preachers talk with the piano behind them.

380
00:24:11,300 --> 00:24:15,960
Preacher stance.

381
00:24:15,960 --> 00:24:29,400
So church, the idea behind Sabbath is not just to set aside time to rest or relax,

382
00:24:29,400 --> 00:24:31,900
but to set aside, oh man, that's good.

383
00:24:31,900 --> 00:24:32,660
Yeah.

384
00:24:32,660 --> 00:24:34,120
Yeah, keep it going.

385
00:24:34,120 --> 00:24:38,620
Oh man, this is great.

386
00:24:38,620 --> 00:24:39,840
Everything I dreamed.

387
00:24:39,840 --> 00:24:48,600
The goal is to reorient our gaze towards a God that loves us and to turn our eyes towards goodness.

388
00:24:48,600 --> 00:24:57,380
And as I was preparing for today's sermon, it struck me again how countercultural this actually is.

389
00:24:58,580 --> 00:25:08,300
That not just setting aside a day to rest, but to set aside a time to orient ourselves towards the goodness in the world and to see the goodness in the world.

390
00:25:09,860 --> 00:25:17,720
Because what I know about the world is that that's not the posture that the world takes towards itself.

391
00:25:17,720 --> 00:25:22,800
And Jesus calls us to be different than the world.

392
00:25:22,800 --> 00:25:26,500
And I'm afraid that we've misinterpreted that over time.

393
00:25:27,320 --> 00:25:37,300
To say our job as Christians is to smash the world into a mold of Christian morality that they haven't accepted as true yet.

394
00:25:38,420 --> 00:25:43,500
And that leaves us at a spot of being frustrated because why can't the non-believers just stop sinning?

395
00:25:44,320 --> 00:25:50,040
And it leads the world to look at us and say, what a bunch of angry hypocrites.

396
00:25:50,040 --> 00:26:03,340
But guys, if we can lay hold of this practice and if we can take this entire day to reorient our gaze and to turn our eyes to the goodness towards the world.

397
00:26:04,340 --> 00:26:10,620
And to recognize that we serve a God and to recognize that we serve a God and we're created in the image of a God who gives us everything that we need.

398
00:26:11,680 --> 00:26:17,840
Then when it's time to re-engage in a world, we can do it with some hope and we can do it with some joy.

399
00:26:17,840 --> 00:26:20,900
And maybe that's the way that we're counter-cultural, right?

400
00:26:20,900 --> 00:26:33,560
I think as Christians, if we can take this posture that God gives us towards the goodness in the world.

401
00:26:33,560 --> 00:26:38,020
That when we walk into a room, everybody should be able to breathe a little deeper.

402
00:26:38,020 --> 00:26:51,980
And that when we engage in important conversations about important topics, that we can do it with an attitude that brings some hope to it.

403
00:26:51,980 --> 00:26:56,240
And so this is my prayer for you, church.

404
00:26:56,240 --> 00:27:03,000
May the God who created the universe and called it good.

405
00:27:05,560 --> 00:27:11,580
May you be an image bearer of Him as you go throughout your week this week.

406
00:27:11,580 --> 00:27:15,960
May you turn your eyes towards good.

407
00:27:15,960 --> 00:27:25,600
And as you turn your eyes towards good, may that fill up your body with light so that when we engage the darkness, we shed light on it.

408
00:27:25,600 --> 00:27:29,400
May God be glorified with that.

409
00:27:29,400 --> 00:27:33,340
May you rest in knowing who you are and who God is.

410
00:27:34,600 --> 00:27:36,120
In Jesus' name, amen.

411
00:27:36,120 --> 00:27:58,000
I'm about to give Brian the biggest compliment

412
00:27:58,000 --> 00:27:59,100
I've ever given anybody,

413
00:27:59,100 --> 00:28:01,760
which is, Brian, as you were talking,

414
00:28:01,760 --> 00:28:03,260
you reminded me of a Bluey episode.

415
00:28:04,600 --> 00:28:12,140
This is the season of pastoral ministry

416
00:28:12,140 --> 00:28:14,820
you all find yourselves in underneath my leadership.

417
00:28:14,820 --> 00:28:18,400
There's an episode of Bluey called Born Yesterday.

418
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:23,840
And Bandit, the dad, spends all day with his kids

419
00:28:23,840 --> 00:28:26,720
pretending like he was born yesterday

420
00:28:26,720 --> 00:28:30,260
and asking them all of the questions

421
00:28:30,260 --> 00:28:32,200
about all of the things that he doesn't know about.

422
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:36,500
And at the end of the episode,

423
00:28:36,500 --> 00:28:38,460
they kind of finish playing the game

424
00:28:38,460 --> 00:28:40,520
and the mom looks out the window

425
00:28:40,520 --> 00:28:43,420
and the dad is sitting on the ground,

426
00:28:43,420 --> 00:28:47,200
cross-legged, staring at a butterfly.

427
00:28:47,200 --> 00:28:50,440
Like it was the first time he's ever seen it before.

428
00:28:50,440 --> 00:28:52,960
And she says, kids,

429
00:28:52,960 --> 00:28:55,100
is your dad still playing Born Yesterday?

430
00:28:56,380 --> 00:28:57,400
And they say, no.

431
00:28:57,400 --> 00:29:01,260
And she looks out at him and just gives like a, hmm.

432
00:29:01,260 --> 00:29:04,280
And as Brian was talking,

433
00:29:04,280 --> 00:29:06,160
I just couldn't help but think that

434
00:29:06,160 --> 00:29:08,400
Sabbath is that opportunity

435
00:29:08,400 --> 00:29:11,900
for us to refresh our eyes

436
00:29:11,900 --> 00:29:13,900
on the goodness of life.

437
00:29:13,900 --> 00:29:16,300
There's so many things that we breeze by

438
00:29:16,300 --> 00:29:17,120
every single day

439
00:29:17,120 --> 00:29:19,440
because of the difficulties and challenges

440
00:29:19,440 --> 00:29:20,780
and anxieties and worries.

441
00:29:20,780 --> 00:29:23,100
There's so many gifts from God

442
00:29:23,100 --> 00:29:27,140
that we walk past, run past, fly past.

443
00:29:27,140 --> 00:29:29,480
And Sabbath is this invitation

444
00:29:29,480 --> 00:29:31,000
to just take a day every week

445
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:32,980
and to set aside the burdens

446
00:29:32,980 --> 00:29:34,380
and the worries and the anxieties

447
00:29:34,380 --> 00:29:39,620
and just believe that this world is good

448
00:29:39,620 --> 00:29:41,780
and that our God is good

449
00:29:41,780 --> 00:29:43,960
and that he's gonna take care of us.

450
00:29:44,800 --> 00:29:46,780
And what might that change

451
00:29:46,780 --> 00:29:48,280
how we look at everything else?

452
00:29:48,280 --> 00:29:51,780
And so I was just, Brian,

453
00:29:51,780 --> 00:29:52,920
so encouraged by that

454
00:29:52,920 --> 00:29:55,460
and challenged by it.

455
00:29:55,460 --> 00:30:00,020
Part of the reason I'm having such a bad day

456
00:30:00,020 --> 00:30:01,820
is my pipes have been frozen

457
00:30:01,820 --> 00:30:03,200
for like 36 hours.

458
00:30:03,200 --> 00:30:07,440
And again, there's a way to look at that

459
00:30:07,440 --> 00:30:10,120
that turns the eye towards darkness.

460
00:30:10,120 --> 00:30:12,820
And there's a way to look at that

461
00:30:12,820 --> 00:30:16,840
that says my most inconvenient situation

462
00:30:16,840 --> 00:30:19,240
is luxury across half of the world.

463
00:30:19,240 --> 00:30:23,160
And Sabbath is this thing

464
00:30:23,160 --> 00:30:25,180
that postures us towards seeing the good,

465
00:30:25,180 --> 00:30:28,300
delighting in the gifts that God gives us.

466
00:30:28,300 --> 00:30:31,020
So I know it's counter-cultural.

467
00:30:31,020 --> 00:30:34,560
